{"step_index":1411,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T12:34:12Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T12:34:12Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T12:34:13Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 234\n1: ---\n2: name: grpc-expert\n3: description: \"Expert guidance for gRPC/Protocol Buffers work in the operation-dbus-proto (OP-DBUS) workspace — designing plugin methods, generating .proto/service definitions, wiring the op-grpc-bridge, or fixing plugins with missing gRPC methods. Use this whenever the user mentions gRPC, proto/protobuf, plugin methods, the gRPC bridge, tonic, reflection descriptors, 'missing plugins' in the gRPC diagnostic page, or the seal/freeze/hot pipeline — even if they don't say 'gRPC' explicitly (e.g. 'the plugin isn't showing up on the diagnostic page', 'add a method to plugin X', 'regen protos').\"\n4: metadata:\n5:   version: 1.0.0\n6: disable-model-invocation: false\n7: ---\n8: \n9: # gRPC Expert — OP-DBUS\n10: \n11: This workspace does **not** hand-write per-plugin `.proto` files. Proto generation is\n12: automatic, driven entirely by each plugin's `PluginSchema`. If you remember one thing\n13: from this skill: **fixing a plugin's gRPC surface means editing its Rust schema\n14: function, not writing `.proto`.**\n15: \n16: ## The real pipeline (roll → seal → freeze → hot)\n17: \n18: ```\n19: 1. Plugin declares  → PluginSchema { methods: HashMap<String, MethodDecl>, signals: Vec<SignalDecl>, ... }\n20:                        in crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/<plugin>.rs\n21: 2. Schema gets put   → op-projection's SchemaEngine publishes the catalog hash\n22:                        (op_identity::schema_bridge::schema_catalog_hash())\n23: 3. Sealed by blob    → op-blob seals the plugin object into an immutable blob:\n24:                        /dev/shm/opdbus/plugin-blobs/<plugin_id>.<schema_hash16>.blob\n25:                        (op-blob is the ONLY writer — never write blobs elsewhere)\n26: 4. Descriptors       → TWO separate reflection layers exist, don't confuse them:\n27:    for reflection       a) STATIC: crates/op-grpc-bridge/build.rs generates\n28:                             plugin_methods.proto from every plugin's schema.methods,\n29:                             compiles it with tonic_build into operation_descriptor.bin\n30:                             (a protobuf FileDescriptorSet), served by tonic-reflection.\n31:                          b) DYNAMIC: crates/op-grpc-bridge/src/dynamic_reflection.rs\n32:                             (ActiveReflectionCatalog) hydrates live from the sealed\n33:                             SHM blob catalog via hydrate_reflection_from_shm() —\n34:                             this is the \"frozen\" descriptor set for what's actually\n35:                             present right now, independent of what's compiled in.\n36: 5. Frozen            → the blob + its descriptors are immutable once sealed.\n37: 6. gRPC socket = hot → crates/op-grpc-bridge/src/grpc_server.rs registers the\n38:                        generated per-plugin service (plugin_method_routes.rs,\n39:                        add_routes()) on the tonic UDS bridge, dispatching each\n40:                        RPC to call_generated_plugin_method_typed(plugin_id,\n41:                        schema_name, ...) which routes through PluginService.CallMethod\n42:                        — i.e. gRPC never bypasses D-Bus, it's a bridge, not a\n43:                        second control plane.\n44: ```\n45: \n46: A plugin shows green/connected on the gRPC diagnostic page **iff**:\n47: - its `PluginSchema.methods` is non-empty (empty → `build.rs` silently skips it —\n48:   see `collect_plugin_methods()`), **and**\n49: - the plugin is sealed in the SHM blob catalog.\n50: "}
{"step_index":1412,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T12:34:12Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T12:34:12Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T12:34:13Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 63\n1: ---\n2: name: grpc-protocol-expert\n3: description: >-\n4:   Generic specialist in gRPC protocol, streaming RPCs, Protocol Buffers, channel\n5:   config, load balancing, auth, and observability. Use for general gRPC/protobuf\n6:   work outside the OP-DBUS PluginSchema seal/freeze/hot pipeline. For OP-DBUS\n7:   plugin gRPC surfaces, prefer the grpc-expert skill instead.\n8: disable-model-invocation: false\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # gRPC Protocol Expert\n12: \n13: ## Focus Areas\n14: \n15: - gRPC protocol intricacies and best practices\n16: - Unary, server-streaming, client-streaming, and bidirectional streaming RPCs\n17: - Protocol Buffers (protobuf) for efficient serialization\n18: - Service definition and implementation in gRPC\n19: - Channel configuration and management\n20: - Load balancing strategies within gRPC\n21: - gRPC authentication and authorization mechanisms\n22: - Network optimization for gRPC communication\n23: - Observability setups, including logging, tracing, and metrics\n24: - Efficient handling of gRPC errors and status codes\n25: \n26: ## Approach\n27: \n28: - Begin with a clear understanding of service requirements before implementing\n29: - Use Protocol Buffers for defining service interfaces and messages\n30: - Implement efficient error handling with gRPC status codes\n31: - Leverage streaming for real-time data processing where applicable\n32: - Optimize network usage by compressing messages and headers\n33: - Employ deadline and timeouts for better control over communication\n34: - Choose appropriate load balancing strategies for scalability\n35: - Configure multiple channels and target services for robustness\n36: - Utilize SSL/TLS for secure communication\n37: - Implement structured logging, tracing, and metrics setup for observability\n38: \n39: ## Quality Checklist\n40: \n41: - Thoroughly defined .proto files adhering to defined conventions\n42: - Service implementation matches the .proto specification\n43: - Correctly configured server and client channels\n44: - Stream types appropriately used based on data flow needs\n45: - Efficient serialization and deserialization processes\n46: - Comprehensive unit and integration testing for gRPC calls\n47: - Implemented error handling with descriptive status codes\n48: - Adequate logging of gRPC requests and responses\n49: - Metrics capturing for latency, error rates, and payload size\n50: - Secure communication ensured with proper encryption standards"}
{"step_index":1413,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T12:34:13Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T12:34:13Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T12:34:13Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 377\n1: ---\n2: name: json-render\n3: description: >-\n4:   Expert skill for building json-render.dev catalog components, page specs, and\n5:   generative UI across three repos: /srv/git/json-render (library source),\n6:   /srv/git/odbus (Rust backend + op-web Axum server), and\n7:   /srv/git/3tched.com (separate chatbot-rendered UI repository).\n8:   Covers the full stack: defineCatalog → Zod schema → React component →\n9:   pageSpec → NAV_MANIFEST → factory plugin wiring → sealed blob pipeline →\n10:   gallery/catalog promotion gate. Also covers the D-Bus plugin schema for\n11:   json-render and schema-renderer in op-plugins.\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # json-render Skill\n15: \n16: ## Local deployment policy — runit, not systemd\n17: \n18: The user-designated UI repository is `/srv/git/3tched.com`, separate\n19: from `/srv/git/odbus`. Inspect its actual catalog, registry and build/sync inputs;\n20: the reference layouts below do not establish its implemented components.\n21: `/srv/git/operation-dashboard-ui-07` and `crates/op-web/ui/` are retired UI paths.\n22: \n23: The host uses runit. Backend runtime actions go through the authenticated D-Bus\n24: plugin surface. Only for authorized operator service work use `sudo -n sv`\n25: (for example `sudo -n sv status op-web`); never use `systemctl`, its shim, s6 or\n26: service6. Edit versioned runit inputs, not `/run/runit/service`. Backend deployment\n27: is btrfs send/receive of an explicitly prepared filesystem snapshot; there is no\n28: deployment script. Never hand-copy binaries. Static UI synchronization does not itself\n29: require an op-web restart. Do not create a parallel frontend backend or expose\n30: broker credentials to the browser.\n31: \n32: EMQX is the retained vendor-binary-backed `emqx` D-Bus plugin, independent of\n33: retired Netmaker. Broker events may drive authorized live state updates while\n34: the chatbot generates schema-pinned layout patches. A generated mode, component\n35: or action never grants permissions: enforce verified session and project ACLs\n36: server-side, including on streamed updates and reconnects.\n37: \n38: ## Overview — Three Repos, One System\n39: \n40: ```\n41: /srv/git/json-render                   ← upstream library (source of truth for API)\n42: /srv/git/odbus                         ← Rust backend\n43:   crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/json_render.rs    D-Bus plugin schema\n44:   crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/schema_renderer.rs\n45:   crates/op-web/src/handlers/ui_model.rs                Axum gallery/catalog routes\n46: /srv/git/3tched.com        ← active UI target; paths below are layout examples\n47:   src/json-render/\n48:     catalog/catalog.ts                 defineCatalog (app catalog contract)\n49:     catalog/registry.tsx               defineRegistry (component implementations)\n50:     catalog/components/                React component render functions"}
{"step_index":1414,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T12:34:13Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T12:34:13Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T12:34:13Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 199\n1: ---\n2: name: unified-memory\n3: description: Share durable, inspectable context and handoffs between Claude, Codex, Hermes, Cursor, OpenCode, and other agents through the local ECC Memory Vault. Use when an agent must save work state, transfer context, resume another agent's task, or search shared project knowledge.\n4: metadata:\n5:   origin: ECC\n6: ---\n7: \n8: # Unified Memory\n9: \n10: Use the ECC Memory Vault as the common context layer between harnesses. The\n11: vault stores portable `ecc.memory.v1` Markdown documents rather than\n12: harness-specific transcripts or inboxes.\n13: \n14: ## Runtime Prerequisite\n15: \n16: This skill is guidance, not the Memory Vault executable. Skill-only, minimal,\n17: manual, and Claude plugin installs do not create the required commands on\n18: `PATH`. Install the `ecc-universal` npm runtime separately before using the CLI\n19: or MCP examples:\n20: \n21: ```bash\n22: npm install -g ecc-universal\n23: ecc memory --help\n24: command -v ecc-memory-mcp\n25: ```\n26: \n27: A repository checkout may instead run the CLI as\n28: `node scripts/ecc.js memory ...`, but MCP configurations that name\n29: `ecc-memory-mcp` still require that binary on `PATH`.\n30: \n31: ## When To Use\n32: \n33: - Save durable context that another agent or later session will need.\n34: - Hand work from Claude to Codex, Hermes to Claude, or any other harness pair.\n35: - Resume a task and search for prior decisions, facts, lessons, or handoffs.\n36: - Diagnose malformed memories, broken links, duplicate IDs, or skipped\n37:   symbolic links.\n38: \n39: Do not use the vault as a task tracker, secret store, policy engine, or\n40: substitute for governed project documentation.\n41: \n42: ## Vault Scopes\n43: \n44: | Scope | Location | Use |\n45: |---|---|---|\n46: | `project` | `<repo>/.ecc/memory/project/` | Repo-local context protected by a fail-closed `.gitignore` |\n47: | `team` | `<repo>/.ecc/memory/team/` | Context intended for human review and version-controlled sharing |\n48: | `user` | `~/.ecc/memory/` | Operator context that follows the user across repositories |\n49: \n50: All participating harnesses must use the same repository working directory or"}
